    "question": {
      ":question/id": "308d897c-3462-5ee5-9ef2-f33a12ed454a",
      ":question/problem": "$\\mathbf{f}(z, x) = ⟨x, 8x^{2}, ?⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(z, x)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $8x^{2} = y$. What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$? Use the named parameters directly as their corresponding Cartesian coordinates.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the named coordinate parameters directly, so the free-axis coordinate is $z.$\n\nFor any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(z,x) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,8{x}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is $z.$",
